## Style

When writing a changeset, keep it customer-oriented and describe the change in a way that is easy to understand. Focus on the user and their needs, not the technical details of the implementation.

Keep it simple and to the point.

If it has no customer impact, keep it extra short.

Breaking changes should be clearly described, and should be accompanied by instructions on how to migrate, particularly for changes to packages which are commonly consumed by customers: `@transcend-io/cli`, `@transcend-io/mcp`, `@transcend-io/airgap.js-types`. When in doubt, ask the user a question to help clarify.

You may optionally link to our [documentation](https://docs.transcend.io/docs/articles) where helpful.

## Updating a changeset

When updating a changeset during a PR, make sure it is NOT logging incremental changes between commits, but rather the overall changes that were made.

